<body>
<sec id="summary">
  <title>Summary</title>
  <p>DataSplits.jl is a Julia package
  (<xref alt="Bezanson et al., 2017" rid="ref-Bezanson2017Julia" ref-type="bibr">Bezanson
  et al., 2017</xref>) for constructing training, validation, test, and
  cross-validation partitions in scientific machine learning workflows.
  While random train/test splits and standard k-fold cross-validation
  are widely available in machine learning libraries, many scientific
  applications require more controlled splitting procedures. The package
  exposes a single entry point:</p>
  <code language="julia">res = partition(data, strategy; kwargs...)</code>
  <p>Supported strategies include distance-based representative sample
  selection for calibration modelling, group-aware validation to avoid
  leakage between related observations, and time-aware validation for
  temporally ordered data. The package is lightweight and interoperable
  with MLUtils.jl, Flux.jl, and MLJ.jl. It can be installed from the
  Julia General Registry with
  <monospace>] add DataSplits</monospace>.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="statement-of-need">
  <title>Statement of need</title>
  <p>The choice of data partitioning strategy can strongly affect the
  reliability of model evaluation, particularly in small-data scientific
  domains such as chemometrics, spectroscopy, metabolomics, materials
  informatics, and quantitative structure–property (QSPR) or
  structure–activity (QSAR) modelling. In these settings, independent
  and identically distributed (i.i.d.) random splits may be
  inappropriate: samples can be highly structured, class distributions
  may be imbalanced, repeated measurements may induce group leakage, and
  calibration sets may need to span the relevant feature and response
  spaces
  (<xref alt="Kohavi, 1995" rid="ref-kohavi1995study" ref-type="bibr">Kohavi,
  1995</xref>). Representative sample selection algorithms such as
  Kennard–Stone/CADEX
  (<xref alt="Kennard &amp; Stone, 1969" rid="ref-Kennard1969Computer" ref-type="bibr">Kennard
  &amp; Stone, 1969</xref>) and SPXY/MDKS
  (<xref alt="Galvão et al., 2005" rid="ref-Galvao2005method" ref-type="bibr">Galvão
  et al., 2005</xref>;
  <xref alt="Saptoro et al., 2012" rid="ref-Saptoro2012Modified" ref-type="bibr">Saptoro
  et al., 2012</xref>) are commonly used in chemometrics to construct
  calibration and validation sets with broad coverage of the
  experimental domain. Related approaches include the Duplex algorithm
  (<xref alt="Snee, 1977" rid="ref-sneeValidation77a" ref-type="bibr">Snee,
  1977</xref>), which selects both training and test sets simultaneously
  using the maximin criterion; OptiSim and dissimilarity-based selection
  methods
  (<xref alt="Clark, 1997" rid="ref-Clark1997OptiSim" ref-type="bibr">Clark,
  1997</xref>); the Onion method
  (<xref alt="Gallagher &amp; O’Sullivan, 2020" rid="ref-gallagherSelection20" ref-type="bibr">Gallagher
  &amp; O’Sullivan, 2020</xref>) and its joint X–y extension, XY-Onion
  (<xref alt="Ezenarro &amp; others, 2025" rid="ref-ezenarro2025xy" ref-type="bibr">Ezenarro
  &amp; others, 2025</xref>); electrostatic field-strength selection
  (<xref alt="He et al., 2026" rid="ref-heField26" ref-type="bibr">He et
  al., 2026</xref>); spectral clustering-based splits
  (<xref alt="Ng et al., 2001" rid="ref-ngSpectral01" ref-type="bibr">Ng
  et al., 2001</xref>); and classification-oriented adaptations such as
  the Morais–Lima–Martin algorithm
  (<xref alt="Lelis Medeiros de Morais et al., 2019" rid="ref-Lelis2019Improving" ref-type="bibr">Lelis
  Medeiros de Morais et al., 2019</xref>). Time-series and financial
  machine learning workflows call for chronological splitting strategies
  such as purged k-fold cross-validation and combinatorial purged
  cross-validation
  (<xref alt="López de Prado, 2018" rid="ref-lopezdepradoAdvances18" ref-type="bibr">López
  de Prado, 2018</xref>), and blocked cross-validation
  (<xref alt="Bergmeir &amp; Benítez, 2012" rid="ref-bergmeirUse12" ref-type="bibr">Bergmeir
  &amp; Benítez, 2012</xref>;
  <xref alt="Roberts et al., 2017" rid="ref-Roberts2017Cross" ref-type="bibr">Roberts
  et al., 2017</xref>). Target-aware cross-validation strategies such as
  Venetian blinds
  (<xref alt="Næs et al., 2002" rid="ref-naes2002multivariate" ref-type="bibr">Næs
  et al., 2002</xref>) assign samples sorted by response value across
  folds in round-robin order, guaranteeing uniform target coverage
  without binning. To our knowledge, these methods are not available
  together in any general-purpose Julia library.</p>
  <p>DataSplits.jl collects them in a single package with a consistent
  interface, for researchers who need reproducible, inspectable, and
  domain-appropriate partitions. For example, Kennard–Stone selection
  constructs a training set that spans the descriptor space, which
  random splits cannot guarantee
  (<xref alt="[fig:ks]" rid="figU003Aks">[fig:ks]</xref>).</p>
  <fig>
    <caption><p>Random and Kennard–Stone training-set selection on a
    synthetic dataset with a dense centre and a sparse boundary. Gray
    points denote all samples (N=120) and blue points the training set
    (n=35). Kennard–Stone covers the boundary of the experimental
    domain, whereas random selection concentrates in the
    centre.<styled-content id="figU003Aks"></styled-content></p></caption>
    <graphic mimetype="image" mime-subtype="png" xlink:href="ks_vs_random.png" />
  </fig>
</sec>
<sec id="state-of-the-field">
  <title>State of the field</title>
  <p>The closest Python equivalents are scikit-learn
  (<xref alt="Pedregosa et al., 2018" rid="ref-pedregosaScikitlearn18" ref-type="bibr">Pedregosa
  et al., 2018</xref>) and astartes
  (<xref alt="Burns et al., 2023" rid="ref-Burns2023Machine" ref-type="bibr">Burns
  et al., 2023</xref>). Scikit-learn provides group-aware and
  time-series CV strategies (<monospace>GroupKFold</monospace>,
  <monospace>TimeSeriesSplit</monospace>,
  <monospace>StratifiedGroupKFold</monospace>) but does not implement
  distance-based selection methods such as Kennard–Stone, SPXY, or
  OptiSim. Astartes fills that gap for molecular and materials data,
  offering Kennard–Stone and several dissimilarity-based splitters, but
  does not cover group-aware CV, time-series CV, or nested
  cross-validation. DataSplits.jl covers both families: distance-based
  selection and a broad catalogue of standard CV strategies. On the
  samplers the two packages share, DataSplits.jl is 1.2 to 27 times
  faster than astartes and uses less peak memory
  (<xref alt="Table 1" rid="tabU003Aastartes">Table 1</xref>). Both
  packages precompute the full distance matrix for distance-based
  samplers, which limits the dataset size that fits in memory;
  DataSplits.jl also provides lazy variants that allow working with data
  of any size.</p>
  <table-wrap>
    <caption>
      <p>Median runtime and peak memory of DataSplits.jl and astartes on
      identical synthetic inputs with 20 features. Scripts and results
      are in <monospace>benchmark/experiment/</monospace>.
      <styled-content id="tabU003Aastartes"></styled-content></p>
    </caption>
    <table>
      <colgroup>
        <col width="32%" />
        <col width="8%" />
        <col width="14%" />
        <col width="11%" />
        <col width="10%" />
