          <td align="right">0.02</td>
          <td align="right">0.04</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </table-wrap>
  <p>Within Julia, MLUtils.jl provides an observation interface based on
  <monospace>numobs</monospace>, <monospace>getobs</monospace>, and lazy
  observation views, together with data loaders and basic splitting
  utilities
  (<xref alt="JuliaML, 2022" rid="ref-mlutils" ref-type="bibr">JuliaML,
  2022</xref>). Flux.jl re-exports MLUtils data-loading functionality
  for neural network training
  (<xref alt="Innes, 2018" rid="ref-Innes2018Flux" ref-type="bibr">Innes,
  2018</xref>). MLJ.jl provides a model-evaluation framework with
  holdout and cross-validation resampling, and also accepts explicit
  train/test row pairs
  (<xref alt="Blaom et al., 2020" rid="ref-Blaom2020MLJ" ref-type="bibr">Blaom
  et al., 2020</xref>). None of these three implement distance-based,
  group-stratified, or purged time-series splitting strategies. The
  closest Julia package in scope is ChemometricsTools.jl
  (<xref alt="Kneale, 2021" rid="ref-chemometricstools" ref-type="bibr">Kneale,
  2021</xref>), a broad chemometrics toolkit offering Kennard–Stone
  selection, two-way <monospace>SplitByProportion</monospace> and
  <monospace>VenetianBlinds</monospace> splits, and
  <monospace>KFoldsValidation</monospace>/<monospace>LeaveOneOut</monospace>
  iterators, all returning materialised arrays, but no group-stratified
  or purged time-series splitting.</p>
  <p>DataSplits.jl complements these packages, its core contribution is
  split construction rather than model evaluation or data loading;
  keeping it lightweight lets the same split definitions be reused
  across MLJ, Flux, MLUtils, and custom workflows.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="software-design">
  <title>Software design</title>
  <p>The central abstraction in DataSplits.jl is a splitting strategy.
  Each strategy is represented by a concrete subtype of
  <monospace>AbstractSplitStrategy</monospace>, while cross-validation
  strategies subtype <monospace>AbstractCVStrategy</monospace>. Users
  call <monospace>partition(data, strategy; kwargs...)</monospace> to
  produce an <monospace>AbstractSplitResult</monospace> (a two-way,
  three-way, or per-fold cross-validation split). A single entry point
  lets user code switch strategies without changing call sites.
  <xref alt="Table 2" rid="tabU003Afamilies">Table 2</xref> lists the
  strategy families.</p>
  <table-wrap>
    <caption>
      <p>Splitting strategy families implemented in DataSplits.jl.
      <styled-content id="tabU003Afamilies"></styled-content></p>
    </caption>
    <table>
      <colgroup>
        <col width="17%" />
        <col width="49%" />
        <col width="34%" />
      </colgroup>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th align="left">Strategy family</th>
          <th align="left">Examples</th>
          <th align="left">Typical use</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td align="left">Distance-based</td>
          <td align="left">KennardStoneSplit, SPXYSplit, OptiSimSplit,
          DuplexSplit, OnionSplit, FieldStrengthSplit,
          SpectralSplit</td>
          <td align="left">Calibration/test design in chemometrics and
          descriptor spaces</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td align="left">Target-aware</td>
          <td align="left">TargetPropertySplit, StratifiedKFold,
          StratifiedShuffleSplit, XYOnionSplit, VenetianBlindsCV</td>
          <td align="left">Class-balanced or response-aware
          validation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td align="left">Group-aware</td>
          <td align="left">GroupShuffleSplit, GroupKFold,
          StratifiedGroupKFold</td>
          <td align="left">Avoiding leakage across related
          observations</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td align="left">Time-aware</td>
          <td align="left">TimeSplit, BlockedCV, PurgedKFold,
          CombinatorialPurgedKFold</td>
          <td align="left">Temporally ordered validation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td align="left">Resampling</td>
          <td align="left">BootstrapSplit, ShuffleSplit, RepeatedKFold,
          NestedCV</td>
          <td align="left">Repeated performance estimation and
          hyperparameter tuning</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </table-wrap>
  <p>The package exposes stable accessors
  (e.g. <monospace>trainindices</monospace>), so downstream code does
  not need to depend on internal result fields. Exposing indices rather
  than only data subsets lets the same split be applied to multiple
  aligned arrays (features, labels, metadata) without re-running the
  selection algorithm. Data can be extracted either as lazy
  MLUtils-compatible observation views or as materialised subsets.</p>
  <p>All distance-based strategies have a lazy counterpart that computes
  distances on-the-fly in O(N) peak memory instead of precomputing the
  full O(N²) distance matrix. This makes the strategies applicable to
  datasets where the full matrix would not fit in RAM, at the cost of
  increased runtime. At N = 10,000 the lazy variants allocate 236 KiB
  where the eager ones allocate 763 MiB, for a 1.4 to 2.4 times increase
  in runtime. Under a 4 GiB budget this raises the largest feasible
  sample count from 10,000 to at least 100,000.</p>
  <p>The package is designed to be extensible. Adding a custom strategy
  requires subtyping <monospace>AbstractSplitStrategy</monospace>,
  declaring which auxiliary slots (<monospace>:data</monospace>,
  <monospace>:target</monospace>, <monospace>:time</monospace>,
  <monospace>:groups</monospace>) the strategy reads via the
  <monospace>consumes</monospace> trait, and implementing a single
  <monospace>_partition</monospace> method. The
  <monospace>partition</monospace> entry point handles cohort-size
  resolution, slot validation, and feature-matrix conversion
  automatically.</p>
  <p>Ecosystem integration is kept lightweight: lazy views can be passed
  directly to Flux/MLUtils data loaders, and
  <monospace>rowpairs</monospace> converts any split result into the
  <monospace>(train_indices, test_indices)</monospace> pairs accepted by
  MLJ’s <monospace>evaluate!</monospace>, without a dependency on
  MLJ.jl.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="research-impact-statement">
  <title>Research impact statement</title>
  <p>DataSplits.jl has been developed to support benchmarking and
  validation workflows in scientific machine learning where split
  construction directly affects reported performance estimates. The
  package underpins a published companion study
  (<xref alt="Crucitti &amp; Mosquera Orgueira, 2026" rid="ref-crucitti2025splits" ref-type="bibr">Crucitti
  &amp; Mosquera Orgueira, 2026</xref>), which demonstrates empirically
  that split strategy choice can dominate differences between modelling
  algorithms in structured chemical datasets, with random splits
  systematically producing more optimistic performance estimates than
  distance-based and group-aware alternatives. A reproducible tutorial
  (<monospace>notebooks/tutorial.jl</monospace>) compares random,
  Kennard–Stone, SPXY, and group-aware splits on controlled datasets,
  quantifying feature-space coverage and group leakage, and generates
  <xref alt="[fig:ks]" rid="figU003Aks">[fig:ks]</xref>. A benchmark
  suite (<monospace>benchmark/benchmarks.jl</monospace>) tracks runtime
  scaling of all strategies. DataSplits.jl will be maintained by the
  GrHeCo-Xen group through the public GitHub repository, where users can
  report issues, request features, and contribute new splitting
  strategies.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="ai-usage-disclosure">
  <title>AI usage disclosure</title>
  <p>Generative AI tools, including Claude Code (Anthropic), were used
  to assist with software development — specifically in the integration
  of some algorithms — documentation drafting, and drafting and language
  editing of this paper. AI tools were not used to make autonomous
  design, implementation, or scientific decisions. All AI-assisted code
  was thoroughly verified against independent reference implementations
  and covered by the test suite. All AI-assisted text was reviewed and
  edited by the authors, and technical claims were checked against the
  implementation, tests, documentation, and cited literature. The
  authors are responsible for the final content of the manuscript, code,
  and documentation.</p>
</sec>
